  "description": "Simulate request token scheduling by interleaving single-token generation decodes with chunked prompt prefills under token batch limits.\n\nUnchunked prefills monopolize computational engines for hundreds of milliseconds. Chunking prompts into uniform token budgets bounds iteration latency while sustaining decode throughput.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nB_{\\text{step}} = N_{\\text{decode}} + \\min(B - N_{\\text{decode}}, L_{\\text{prompt\\_rem}})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Allocate 1 decode token to active requests with 0 prompt tokens and positive decode tokens remaining\n2. Allocate remaining token budget to slice prompt tokens of arriving or ongoing prefill requests\n3. Record executed operations per scheduling cycle until all requests finish\n\n### Returns\nList of step execution lists containing request id, prefill_tokens, and decode_tokens.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Simulate Chunked Prefill Scheduling\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRunning large AI models in production requires massive computing power. During text generation, memory bandwidth (fetching weights and past key-value caches from VRAM) is often the primary bottleneck rather than raw arithmetic compute. **Simulate Chunked Prefill Scheduling** optimizes hardware efficiency, memory consumption, or request serving throughput.\n\nUnchunked prefills monopolize computational engines for hundreds of milliseconds. Chunking prompts into uniform token budgets bounds iteration latency while sustaining decode throughput. In this problem, the goal is to: simulate request token scheduling by interleaving single-token generation decodes with chunked prompt prefills under token batch limits.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nB_{\\text{step}} = N_{\\text{decode}} + \\min(B - N_{\\text{decode}}, L_{\\text{prompt\\_rem}})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Allocate 1 decode token to active requests with 0 prompt tokens and positive decode tokens remaining\n2. **Phase 2**: Allocate remaining token budget to slice prompt tokens of arriving or ongoing prefill requests\n3. **Phase 3**: Record executed operations per scheduling cycle until all requests finish\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Memory Bandwidth Bottleneck**: Quantizing weights to INT8 or INT4 cuts memory traffic in half or quarters, dramatically increasing token generation speeds.\n* **Serving Systems**: Dynamic batching and PagedAttention maximize GPU utilization by grouping requests arriving at different times.\n* **Latency vs. Throughput**: In production serving, systems engineers balance Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) against overall tokens-per-second throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: requests = [{\"id\": 1, \"prompt_tokens_remaining\": 5, \"decode_tokens_remaining\": 2}, {\"id\": 2, \"prompt_tokens_remaining\": 2, \"decode_tokens_remaining\": 1}], max_tokens_per_batch = 4\n\nOutput: [[{\"id\": 1, \"prefill_tokens\": 4, \"decode_tokens\": 0}], [{\"id\": 1, \"prefill_tokens\": 1, \"decode_tokens\": 0}, {\"id\": 2, \"prefill_tokens\": 2, \"decode_tokens\": 0}], [{\"id\": 1, \"prefill_tokens\": 0, \"decode_tokens\": 1}, {\"id\": 2, \"prefill_tokens\": 0, \"decode_tokens\": 1}], [{\"id\": 1, \"prefill_tokens\": 0, \"decode_tokens\": 1}]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated inference_chunked_prefill_scheduling under standard test conditions."
